  "worked_solution": "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x-\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x)+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x)=R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x-\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x)=(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)+(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5}{R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}\u2061\u03d5 & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4} \\\\ \u03d5 & =arctan\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})\u22480.510\\text{\u00a0}\\text{rad},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)^{2}+(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)^{2} & ={4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\u03d5+{sin}^{2}\u2061\u03d5) & =16+5 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =21 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6x)\u2248\\sqrt{21}\\,cos\\,(6x-0.510).\n$$",
